encyclopedia :olddowns do
  name "Olddowns"
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :anomaly
  status :complete
  log "2026-08-31 — Renamed Hounds to Orients; the title names their inherited directional sense."
  log "2026-08-31 — Renamed Orients to Olddowns. Restoration crews call the ring's original gravity direction old down; the animals embody and reveal that setting-specific bearing rather than merely possessing orientation."
  topics :"fluid-reality", :"ring-era", :mystery, :ecology
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when any: { place: [:sealed_hab, :debris_field] }
  summary "Olddowns are lean dog-shaped anomalies whose weight follows the original ring orientation of each section they enter. They walk former floors through changed gravity, rest at old corners, and pause where vanished doorways once opened."
  origin "Documented in hab galleries and fragment interiors since the earliest postfall records; the survey literature's founding observation — that every olddown's private down matches the ring's original orientation for that section — has held through every survey since"
  biology "A lean, short-furred, dog-shaped animal by every external measure, whose weight simply answers a different down — it stands on whatever surface the ring's original orientation makes a floor, walks spin-country walls at an unhurried trot, and rests curled in corners that were corners once"
  lifespan "Unestablished; marked individuals persist across survey generations, and no observed birth, growth stage, or death fixes the limits"
  function "An olddown's stance gives the original ring alignment of its current section; restoration trades compare that bearing with recovered plans and structural surveys"
  resonance_relation "The animal presses its chosen surface with ordinary weight, but instruments have not detected the force that holds it to the section's former down"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "A lean, short-furred, dog-shaped animal in gallery gray, " \
                "encountered mid-wall or overhead at an unhurried trot — " \
                "level, comfortable, and oriented to a floor that stopped " \
                "being one at the Glassfall.",
    behavior: "It follows the ring's original alignment wherever it goes — " \
              "walking old floors through new gravities, resting in " \
              "corners that were corners, drinking, when it drinks, from " \
              "water it laps sideways off a seep — and it moves through " \
              "inhabited country on long repeated rounds.",
    threat: "An olddown carries ordinary weight on an unexpected surface. People " \
            "below its overhead route clear loose objects and give it room at " \
            "junctions, where two definitions of floor can cross.",
    senses: "Olddowns thread changed sections along courses matching old deck " \
            "plans and stop at the former position of doorways even when the " \
            "frame is buried or gone.",
    risks: "An olddown may wait for days at a sealed former passage. Restoration " \
           "crews verify the old plan and inspect the present structure before " \
           "deciding whether to reopen it."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    An olddown is lean, short-furred, dog-shaped, and aligned to the ring's former down — old down in the restoration trades. In a fragment whose tumble made walls of floors, it walks along the surface that served as floor before the Glassfall. Its paws press with ordinary weight. Surveys have not detected the holding force, but repeated measurements place the stance to the original orientation of each section.

    Olddowns travel long rounds through galleries and wreck interiors. They rest in former corners, lap seep water from the side that used to lie below it, and pass through old door positions in the sequence shown on recovered plans. At a blocked or vanished doorway, an animal may wait for days before reversing its route. Marked individuals repeat these paths across survey generations. Birth, growth, feeding, and death remain undocumented in the available observation record.

    Restoration crews use an olddown's stance to check recovered plans and follow its rounds to identify lost corridor geometry. Hab residents leave resting corners clear and route traffic around an animal walking overhead. When an olddown waits at a sealed passage, crews inspect both the old plan and the present structure. Some reopen the former doorway when it can be done safely; others mark the position and leave it closed.
  PROSE

  cue "The olddown trots overhead down the gallery's wall at its unhurried pace — level to a floor that stopped being one at the Glassfall — and foot traffic bends around its route with the ease of long custom."
  cue "The restoration crew checks the rebuilt junction the trade's way — asking the dog — and the olddown's stance, set against the new decking, reads two degrees of error the instruments missed."
  affordance "An olddown's stance preserves the former down of each ring section, while its repeated route marks old corridors and doorway positions for comparison with surveys."
  variation "Gallery olddowns range settled habs and are known individually; the fragment-wreck populations of the debris fields walk old alignments through open ruin, and salvage crews chart their rounds as free survey."
  variation "Survey literature compares relict maintenance fauna and a postfall anomaly as origin models. The measured alignment and repeated rounds support neither model by themselves."
end
